.\" Process this file with
.\" groff -man -Tascii sudosh.conf.5
.\"

.TH SUDOSH.CONF 5 "December 21th 2007" @VERSION@ "User Manuals"
.SH NAME
sudosh.conf \- configuration file for sudosh
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sudosh.conf
is used to configure sudosh.  The log directory, logging parameters and other default entries are stored within this file.
A \fB#\fR starts a comment that runs to the end of the line, on its own line or after a value. White space is ignored, so values cannot contain spaces or a \fB#\fR.
.SH OPTIONS
.IP logdir
.RS
logdir = /path/to/logging/directory
Use this to set the default logging directory.  Both sudosh and sudosh-replay will use this to deteremine the logging directory.

.PP
The directory is created with mode 1733: anyone can add recordings to it, but the sticky bit stops users deleting or renaming each other's recordings. An existing directory has its mode corrected each time sudosh or sudosh-replay runs. A path that exists but is not a directory is refused, and its permissions are not changed.
.RE
.IP "default shell"
.RS
default shell = /path/to/default/shell
This is the default shell sudosh will use if it cannot automatically determine which shell to use.
.RE
.IP "immutable_recordings"
.RS
immutable_recordings = (enabled|disabled)
If enabled sets the append only flag on new recordings and changes the recording to
immutable once it is complete. Only available on Linux, default disabled.
.RE
.IP delimiter
.RS
delimiter = -
This is the character you want to delimit the logging files with.  For example some environments may use ths underscore "_" or dash "-" in the login names.  If this is the case use the delimiter option to change the logging delimiter to another character such as a period "." or comma ","  The delimiter must be 1 character in length and cannot be alphanumeric.
.RE
.IP syslog.priority
.RS
syslog.priority = PRIORITY
Use this option to set which syslog priority sudosh will use.  The default is LOG_INFO
.RE
.IP syslog.facility
.RS
syslog.facility = FACILITY
Use this option to set which syslog facility sudosh will use.  The default is LOG_LOCAL2
.RE
.IP "-c arg allow"
.RS
-c arg allow = command
This option can be used multiple times.  If you wish to use sudosh as a default login shell and want to allow users to copy files, you can allow them to use commands such as rsync, scp, and sftp.  For each command you want to allow, use a new line and option. To allow all commands, use the special value of *. A command is allowed when its first word matches an entry. The entries are stored in a fixed buffer of about 8 KB in total; sudosh refuses to start if they do not fit.
.PP
\fBWarning:\fR only the command name is checked, not its arguments. A command is run with all the arguments the user gives it (apart from the shell characters \fB; & | < > ` $ ( )\fR and newlines, which are refused unless the list contains \fB*\fR). An allowed program can therefore be used to run other programs or write files if it has options that do so, for example \fBfind -exec\fR and \fBfind -fprint\fR, \fBrsync -e\fR, \fBscp -S\fR and \fBscp -o ProxyCommand=\fR, or \fBtar --to-command\fR. Only allow programs whose options you have reviewed, and treat the list as a convenience, not as a security boundary.
.PP
Note: Modern OpenSSH (9.0+) uses the SFTP protocol by default for scp. If scp or SFTP clients (e.g., WinSCP, FileZilla) fail to connect through sudosh, ensure the sftp-server path is correct and that \fBsftp-server\fR (or \fBinternal-sftp\fR) is available. You may need to add:
.PP
-c arg allow = /usr/lib/openssh/sftp-server
.PP
The sftp-server path varies by distribution. Use \fBfind / -name sftp-server\fR to locate it. For legacy scp behavior, clients can use \fBscp -O\fR (uppercase O) to force the old RCP protocol.
.RE
.IP clearenvironment
.RS
clearenvironment = false
Set this option to true to clear shell environment variables for incoming sessions. Set to false for things like ssh agent forwarding. This also applies to commands run with \fB-c\fR, which otherwise get only \fBHOME\fR, \fBLOGNAME\fR, \fBUSER\fR and a fixed \fBPATH\fR.

.SH FILE TRANSFER
Allowing scp, sftp or rsync with \fB-c arg allow\fR is convenient, but the list checks only the command name (see the warning above). For users who only need to transfer files, it is safer to set this up in \fBsshd_config\fR(5) and not give them a sudosh login at all:
.IP \(bu 3
sftp: use OpenSSH's \fBinternal-sftp\fR with \fBChrootDirectory\fR and \fBForceCommand internal-sftp\fR in a \fBMatch\fR block for those users. They are confined to one directory, with no shell and no command line.
.IP \(bu 3
rsync: use the \fBrrsync\fR script that ships with rsync, as \fBcommand="rrsync /path"\fR in the user's \fBauthorized_keys\fR. It checks both the options and the paths. It reads \fBSSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND\fR, which sshd sets only for \fBcommand=\fR, so it cannot be used through \fB-c arg allow\fR.
.IP \(bu 3
a single fixed job: put \fBcommand="..."\fR in \fBauthorized_keys\fR, so the client cannot choose the command.
.PP
Keep sudosh for interactive sessions that should be recorded.
.SH EXAMPLES
# Sudosh Configuration File
.RE
logdir                  = /var/log/sudosh
.RE
immutable_recordings    = enabled
.RE
default shell           = /bin/sh
.RE
delimiter               = -
.RE
syslog.priority         = LOG_INFO
.RE
syslog.facility         = LOG_LOCAL2
.RE

# Allow Sudosh to execute -c arguements?  If so, what?
.RE
-c arg allow = scp
.RE
-c arg allow = rsync
.RE
-c arg allow = /usr/lib/openssh/sftp-server
.RE
-c arg allow = /usr/libexec/openssh/sftp-server


# Don't preserve environment variables
.RE 
clearenvironment = yes

.SH AUTHOR
Josh Grebe <josh@shortcutsolutions.net>
Douglas Richard Hanks, Jr. <dhanks@gmail.com>
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR sudosh-replay (8),
.BR sudo (8)
